#import "style.typ": *
#set par(leading: 0.585em, spacing: 0.64em)
#chapter[A common interval for the cohomology]

== The statement with its quantifiers <c16s01>

In this theorem $x$ is the raw scale, denoted $x'$ when compared with the physical scale in Chapter 18. Fix a primitive character $psi$, $0<epsilon<1/52$, and any function $e:ℝ arrow.r ℕ$ satisfying $x^(2+8epsilon)<=2^(e(x))<=2x^(2+8epsilon)$ for every $x>=1$. There exist $J>=1$ and $X_0$ for this choice. For every $X>=X_0$, set $P=X^(1/J)$ and take any pair of primes $q,d$ in the logarithmic size ranges at $P$. The pair must satisfy $q equiv 3 mod 8$, $d equiv 7 mod 16$, $q divides d+1$, and $p divides d+1$ for every odd prime $p<=P^(epsilon/5)$. These are the hypotheses on the pair in the cohomological statement. Chapter 18 chooses a pair satisfying the additional character estimates as well. After $X,q,d$ have been specified, there exists $Y$ with
$ X^(1/J)<=Y, quad Y^2<=X, $
such that the following estimate holds for every $x in [Y,Y^2]$ and every field $E$ with the fixed-field description for $psi,e(x),q,d$. Put $t_x=floor(x^401)$. Each length with $1<=j<t_x$ is finite and for every finite set of primes $S$,
$ sum_(ℓ in S)(log ℓ)sum_(j=1)^(t_x-1) len_(ℤ_ℓ) Sha_(ℓ)(E,j)
        <=sqrt(exp(x^epsilon)). $
The exponent $401$ is a convenient common cutoff for the ranks used in this construction. The cost $sqrt(b)$ is small compared with the exponential weight $b$. The quantifier over $x$ allows the field and the number of twists to change *within* the interval.

== Counting a family of finite groups <c16s02>

To read the window theorem, consider first one coefficient prime $ℓ$, one number field $E$, and one positive twist $j$. Write $M_j=(ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ)(-j)$. Cohomology classes in the restriction kernel of Chapter 6 are unramified outside the permitted places and have zero restriction at the places over $ℓ$. Two choices made in defining the cohomology, an algebraic closure and local decomposition data, do not supply any freedom to select particularly small groups. The bound in the release quantifies over every such choice. Its arithmetic content is a bound on invariants of $E$.

Let $a_(ℓ,j)$ be the composition length of that kernel over $ℤ_ℓ$. Until finiteness has been proved its values belong to $ℕ union {infinity}$. For an integer $t>=1$ introduce the finite subtotal
$ C_(E)(S,t)=sum_(ℓ in S) (log ℓ) sum_(1<=j<t) a_(ℓ,j). $
If all the indicated lengths are finite, this is the logarithm of the product of the orders of the kernels. Indeed, the sole simple module over the local ring $ℤ_ℓ$ is its residue field of order $ℓ$. A composition series of length $a$ therefore gives order $ℓ^a$. Taking logarithms converts products into the same sums that occur in a determinant. This explains why bounding the number of nonzero groups would leave out information the slope argument needs.

For each fixed field and cutoff the formal estimate has two clauses. It first establishes finiteness of every length in range, and then bounds every finite subtotal by the same number $B$. Finite support for that field follows by the argument at the end of Chapter 15. If $t'<=t$ and $B'>=B$, restriction to twists $1<=j<t'$ preserves finiteness, and deleting nonnegative summands preserves the bound. In particular this monotonicity uses actual finiteness before the extended lengths are converted to natural numbers. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.ExistsPageN")[The two clauses and the monotonicity theorem.]

The index $j$ in this chapter is a twist, while the integer indexing the two-power layer is the value of a function of the raw scale. We use $h$ for that layer index during the calculations below. Set $M$ equal to the conductor of the fixed primitive target and write $q,d$ for its auxiliary primes. An integer which realizes the field inside a cyclotomic extension is
$ N=op("lcm")(2^(h+2),M) q d. $
On the units modulo this integer one imposes four conditions. The component modulo $2^(h+2)$ is one or minus one, the target character takes value one, and the two quadratic characters at $q,d$ both take value one. Their common subgroup fixes $E$. This description continues to make sense after passage to a larger cyclotomic field through which all the characters factor. At a given raw scale the theorem covers every realization of the resulting field. The eventual bounds can thus be applied to the field constructed by the separate arithmetic argument.

There are three sizes here. The conductor of the abelian field divides an integer of the size of $N$. Its degree is at most its cyclotomic conductor. The cutoff $t$ can be a very high power of the raw scale, though its logarithm grows only like the logarithm of that scale. In contrast the budget assigned to the lengths is exponentially large in a small power of the raw scale. Distinguishing degree, conductor, and cutoff makes it possible to see exactly which crude arithmetic inequalities can be afforded.

== Dividing the cohomological count <c16s03>

Because the relevant fields are abelian over $ℚ$, the proof organizes the restrictions by characters of the cyclotomic Galois group. A character of order prime to $ℓ$ can be projected out by a Fourier idempotent over suitable $ℓ$-adic coefficients. Its denominator is the group order on that part, hence a unit. When an $ℓ$-part is present the analogous denominator is not a unit. Integral filtrations and estimates for extension classes replace that decomposition. This is why a proof using only complex characters would miss the possible torsion.

For the plus contribution, fix a finite set $B_psi$ of exceptional odd primes depending only on $psi$. It contains three and the relevant prime divisors of its order, conductor, and auxiliary invariants. For each positive twist $j$ the proof distinguishes whether $ℓ-1$ divides $2j$. This is the test for the corresponding even power of the mod-$ℓ$ cyclotomic character to be trivial. In several subcases the parity test applies to the character left after removing the $ℓ$-local component of its prime-to-$ℓ$ part. The outside-bad-set odd-order case tests the original character order. The principal cases and the sources of their bounds are as follows.

+ Outside $B_psi$, with $ℓ-1 nmid 2j$, a Kato Euler-system estimate, local comparison, and unramified automorphy give a weighted count of the form $(N t)^C$. Here $N$ is the host conductor, $t$ a twist cutoff, and $C$ depends on the target character.
+ For $j>=2$ and even order of the corrected prime-to-$ℓ$ character in the exceptional or divisible range, the integral column bounds come from adjoint lifting and symplectic rank-four image estimates. A second bound treats the nontrivial characters of odd order outside $B_psi$ when $ℓ-1$ divides $2j$. The congruence constructions in these two cases control integral multiplicities as well as rational dimensions.
+ At primes in $B_psi$ with $j>=2$ a fixed-layer estimate covers the case where the *corrected* prime-to-$ℓ$ character has odd order. For $j=1$ inside $B_psi$ the comparison uses the conductor of the two-primary component. The first range includes the equality in $R_1<=2^(c_2)$, and its complement has $2^(c_2)<R_1$. For a trivial character outside $B_psi$ in the divisible case, the vanishing input applies at $ℓ>=5$ and positive even $j>=2$. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.DirichletPrimeToLPartMulLPowerPart")[The corrected character and the six cases.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.Counts")[The two conductor ranges and trivial-character vanishing.]

These specialized estimates are proved subtheorems of the construction. Their common output after summation is particularly simple. For every sufficiently large admissible host, if the required finite list of fixed layers has bound $B$, the plus contribution is bounded by
$ (N t)^C+C B. $
The minus contribution has a separate conductor, degree, and twist estimate using annihilator and character relations of Stickelberger type. Its bound holds independently of the good-window choice. The two-primary tower and fixed bad odd primes are treated by the tower argument. The division into cases is designed so that the set requiring control by fixed layers is independent of how far out $x$ is taken.

== Why the two-primary contribution needs its own argument <c16s04>

Suppose $E$ is CM and write $c$ for the action on cohomology induced by complex conjugation of the field. The collection of local restrictions defining the kernel is stable under $c$, and $c^2=1$. The sign convention for a twist matters. In the release the plus submodule at twist $j$ is the eigenspace in the kernel for the eigenvalue $s=(-1)^j$. The minus submodule is the eigenspace for $-s$. An odd twist therefore exchanges the *names* of the ordinary eigenvalues $+1$ and $-1$.

#claim[Splitting a stable kernel][Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $D$ an $R$-module, $c$ an $R$-linear involution on $D$, and $A$ a $c$-stable submodule. Suppose that $2$ is a unit of $R$ and that $s in R$ satisfies $s^2=1$. Then $A$ is the direct sum of its $s$ and $-s$ eigenspaces for $c$. Its length is the sum of their lengths, including the case of extended lengths.]

#proof[For $a in A$ take $a_+=(a+s c(a))/2$ and $a_-=(a-s c(a))/2$. Both lie in $A$. Applying $c$ and using $s^2=1$ gives $c(a_+)=s a_+$ and $c(a_-)=-s a_-$. Their sum is $a$. If a vector lies in both eigenspaces, then $2s$ annihilates it. Multiplication by $2s$ is invertible. The two constructions thus give inverse module homomorphisms between $A$ and the direct sum. Additivity of length gives the assertion.]

#example[Parity and integral splitting][On $ℤ slash 9ℤ ⊕ ℤ slash 3ℤ$ let $c$ act by minus one on the first summand and by one on the second. When $j$ is odd the plus length is two and the minus length is one. The total is three in either parity. Now let $c$ interchange the two coordinates on $(ℤ slash 4ℤ)^2$. The fixed diagonal and the anti-diagonal each have order four. They intersect in the two elements $(0,0),(2,2)$. Their sum has order eight, although the ambient module has order sixteen. This is an integral instance of the failure of the two projector formulas at two.]

For odd $ℓ$, the preceding lemma applies with $R=ℤ_ℓ$. It reduces a bound for the entire kernel to bounds for the two signed lengths. At two the construction estimates the entire two-primary length by separate tower arguments. If $T_2$ bounds its sum over twists *without* the coefficient $log 2$, and $T_+$ and $T_-$ bound the odd-prime signed sums *with* their logarithmic coefficients, then a bound for every subtotal is
$ (log 2) T_2+T_++T_-. $
To check this inequality for an arbitrary $S$, discard the two-primary term when $2$ is absent and use the odd-prime decomposition separately for every remaining summand. The constants for the three components do not require the same normalization. The factor $log 2$ records the difference. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LengthEqAddEigen")[The twist signs and the integral length formula.]

The minus estimate will later hold at every eligible point of the outer range. The more delicate interval choice is shared by the two-primary estimate and the plus estimate. In the latter, a finite family of odd-prime layers supplies some of the information that the character-by-character count lacks. This determines the order of proof. One first establishes an implication for *any* specified interval on which the finite layers satisfy their bound. One then constructs an interval satisfying its hypothesis.

== Organizing the odd-prime plus count <c16s05>

An abelian character on a finite unit group can be decomposed in two different ways. At a coefficient prime $ℓ$ one may separate its $ℓ$-power *order* from its prime-to-$ℓ$ order. At a prime $p$ dividing the modulus one may instead select its local *conductor component*. A local component at $p$ can have order divisible by another prime $ℓ$, since the unit group modulo $p$ has order $p-1$. The counts in this argument use both operations.

For the first operation let a character $xi$ have order $D=ℓ^a u$ with $(ℓ,u)=1$. Choose an integer $b$ that is one modulo $ℓ^a$ and zero modulo $u$. Then $xi^b$ has $ℓ$-power order, $xi^(1-b)$ has order prime to $ℓ$, and their product is $xi$. Replacing $b$ by another solution leaves both characters fixed. For the second operation write the units of the modulus as a product of its prime-power unit groups by the Chinese remainder theorem. Its $p$-component sends a unit to the value of $xi$ on its $p$-coordinate, with all other coordinates set equal to one. For the parity test used below let $xi'$ denote the prime-to-$ℓ$ order part and put
$ xi^♭=xi' (xi'_ℓ)^(-1), $
where $xi'_ℓ$ is the local *conductor* component of $xi'$ at $ℓ$. The parity of the order of this corrected character appears in the conditions for certain columns of the estimate. The distinction vanishes in some branches as a consequence of their hypotheses. These are separate deductions about the arithmetic host. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.DirichletPrimeToLPartMulLPowerPart")[Character projections and the case definitions.]

Consider, for example, a hypothetical character of order twelve at $ℓ=3$. The integer four is one modulo three and zero modulo four. Thus $xi^4$ has order three and $xi^(-3)$ has order four. Either part could be ramified at a divisor of the modulus different from three. The above factorization says exactly which powers of the *values* occur. Computing the local component at three requires the character's values on the units in that coordinate. This small example explains why a table based on the order of the full character alone would not distinguish all the estimates in the proof.

For the fixed target $psi$ there is a set $B_psi$ of odd coefficient primes. It contains three and the odd prime divisors of a product of five fixed integers, namely the order and modulus of the target and the three auxiliary invariants defining the quadratic-field choices. The important fact about $B_psi$ is that it can be written down before choosing the scale or the pair $q,d$. The number of occurrences of a term attached to a prime of $B_psi$ can therefore be bounded by a constant depending on $psi$. A set of primes defined using the full moving conductor would have a different dependence.

For a fixed twist $j$ the characters counted have the parity condition $xi(-1)=(-1)^j$ and factor through the quotient defining the field. Use a finite extension of $ℤ_ℓ$ large enough for the character values. The source transports the normalized lengths across suitable changes in coefficient ring. This permits the counts to use characters with a common kernel or coefficients admitting the necessary roots of unity. The resulting estimates include separately both finiteness and a bound for a normalized length. A proof involving dimensions of eigenspaces over an algebraic closure of $ℚ_ℓ$ alone would lose the amount of torsion carried by the integral classes.

The divisibility test in the cases is $ℓ-1 divides 2j$. The mod-$ℓ$ cyclotomic character over $ℚ$ has image of order $ℓ-1$, so its $2j$th power is trivial precisely under that condition. When the test holds one also has $ℓ<=2j+1$. Consequently all the primes satisfying it for a given $j$ lie in a finite interval controlled by $t$. The complementary case contains primes with no such size restriction. An estimate that works for each prime with a growing factor of $ℓ$ would be insufficient on that complement. The general-position estimate is a *simultaneous* estimate over an arbitrary finite set of those primes.

Here is a closer account of the proof's division into cases. The names and roles of the inputs will be useful when checking exactly where the local-to-global theorem of Chapter 15 is used.

+ Outside $B_psi$, if $ℓ-1 nmid 2j$, the general-position count bounds the logarithmically weighted sum over a finite set of such primes and all eligible characters by $(N t)^C$. Its proof uses a Kato-type input, the comparison for the relevant Selmer conditions, and automorphy with the prescribed unramified coefficients. To indicate the scale of an intermediate estimate, for a single kernel orbit and fixed imaginary quadratic auxiliary field $K$ the bound has a factor $|D_K|^5 N^18 j^7$ times the totient of a kernel index and the sum of the logs of two auxiliary primes. The orbit estimate has hypotheses at both auxiliary primes and on the residue character. The theorem for the entire general-position case supplies these hypotheses and absorbs the factors into a fixed power. Quoting that theorem retains its uniformity over the finite prime set. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.Counts")[Orbit estimate and general-position count.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.VwinOfLeaves")[Its Kato and automorphic inputs.]
+ Suppose $j>=2$, the order of $xi^♭$ is even, and either $ℓ$ is in $B_psi$ or $ℓ-1 divides 2j$. There is a column estimate for each such character. Outside $B_psi$ there is another column estimate in the divisible case when $xi$ is nontrivial of odd order. These two inputs control normalized length times $log ℓ$ by a fixed power of $ℓ N j$. The control of integral congruences in these branches uses automorphic lifting and the rank-four image argument. The simple inequality $ℓ<=2t+1$ in the divisible branch is what permits an estimate involving $ℓ$ to be summed. Inside $B_psi$ the prime is already bounded in terms of fixed data.
+ For $ℓ$ in $B_psi$ and $j>=2$, the remaining characters have corrected character $xi^♭$ of odd order. Here the estimate uses a fixed layer over the cyclotomic base of conductor $ℓ M$. Its bound for the sum *over twists* has the shape $B+(N t)^C$, where $B$ bounds that layer's sum. The fact that $B$ appears once for this sum is important. A pointwise-in-$j$ bound of $B$ used $t$ times would give $t B$, and would miss the quantitative statement used in the assembly. When $j=1$ inside $B_psi$, two estimates are separated by a conductor threshold comparing a specified size parameter with the two-primary conductor of the character. This avoids applying the $j>=2$ lifting statement at its excluded endpoint. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.LayerLe")[Fixed-layer estimate and its order of summation.]
+ Outside $B_psi$ the remaining trivial-character case with $ℓ-1 divides 2j$ is controlled by vanishing of the indicated auxiliary group when $ℓ>=5$ and $j$ is positive and even with $j>=2$. Its parity is forced because a trivial character is even. Since $3 in B_psi$ and $ℓ$ is odd, these prime hypotheses are met. The source proves that these conditions cover the eligible characters, including the restrictions on their $ℓ$-local components outside $B_psi$. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.Cases")[Exhaustion of the cases.]

As an example of the arithmetic in the split, temporarily suppose $11$ lies outside the fixed bad set and consider $j=3$. The inequality $10 nmid 6$ puts all eligible characters at eleven in the simultaneous count, irrespective of the parity of their order. At $j=10$ one has $10 divides 20$. A trivial character is eligible by its even parity and is in the vanishing case. A nontrivial character of odd order, if present in the host, is in the second column case. Thus even for one coefficient prime there is no permanent assignment to a single arithmetic method through all twists.

== Summing the character bounds before choosing a point <c16s06>

Write $A=N t$. In the range in question $A>=3$. For each odd prime outside $B_psi$, split the contribution into the general-position part, the two divisible column parts and the trivial part. In the general-position branch the uniform estimate applies separately for each twist, and the extra factor $t$ from summing those estimates can be absorbed in a larger fixed power of $A$. In a divisible branch $ℓ<=2t+1$. There are at most $2t+1$ such primes, at most $t-1$ twists, and at most the order of the character group of the modulus many characters. That order is bounded by $N$. Consequently a fixed power of $ℓ N j$ remains a fixed power of $A$ after all three sums. These coarse counts explain why the more precise exponents in the intermediate cases do not propagate all the way to the final exponent $9epsilon$.

For the fixed bad primes the source obtains the implication
$ "fixed-layer bound" B quad arrow.r quad
       "plus bound at one bad prime" <= A^C+C B. $
One point behind this implication deserves care. A fixed-layer term may control sums over all twists of a single character. Freely bounding every member of a large family by that same layer estimate could attach an unwanted power of $A$ to $B$. The proof first singles out a set of exceptional characters of controlled size. For the other characters it uses the polynomial estimates instead. The finite summation underlying this choice can be written as follows.

#claim[A finite splitting estimate][Fix integers $t>=1$, $n>=0$, $q_0>=0$ and real constants $L,K,S_0>=0$. For each integer $1<=j<t$ let $T_j$ have cardinality at most $n$, and let $O$ have cardinality at most $q_0$. Suppose $v(i,j)>=0$, $L v(i,j)<=K$ on $T_j backslash O$, and $sum_(1<=j<t) v(i,j)<=S_0$ for every $i in O$. Then
$ L sum_(1<=j<t) sum_(i in T_j) v(i,j)
   <= L q_0 S_0 + t n K. $]

#proof[Split at membership in $O$. Outside $O$ count at most $t n$ terms, each at most $K$ after multiplication by $L$. For terms in $O$, interchange the two finite sums. For a fixed $i$ the twists with $i in T_j$ form a subset of all the twists. Nonnegativity bounds the sum on that subset by $S_0$. Summation over $O$ gives the first summand.]

One may take $L=log ℓ$ in this model calculation. The release bounds the logarithmic coefficient times the number of exceptional characters at a fixed bad prime in terms of the fixed family in the required branch. Polynomial terms involving the modulus and cutoff are charged to the other summand. Finally sum over the fixed set $B_psi$. Increase $C$ to dominate its size and all its finitely many constants. Together with the estimate outside $B_psi$ this yields the plus estimate with the same two types of cost. It applies uniformly to the finite set used in the definition of a subtotal. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.FinalLe")[Finite splitting and the estimates at bad primes.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.HeightAndVanishing.PointArith")[Assembly of the plus estimate.]

The input from the previous chapter enters this count at a different place from its use in comparing congruence lattices. In the congruence comparison it identifies the Galois data attached to specific eigensystems. In the window theorem the automorphic and local-to-global inputs are used to bound the possible classes *across* characters and twists. These consequences can both be drawn from the same local-to-global theorem. The deduction of the window theorem takes that theorem as an input before any of the favorable intervals have been selected. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.ModularityArgument.ThresholdArguments.DenseThresholdCases.VwinOfRow")[The deduction from the local-to-global theorem.]

== The source of exceptional indices <c16s07>

For a totally imaginary field $K$ containing the relevant roots of unity, a coarse estimate first gives finiteness of $H^(2)(G_(K,S),ℤ_(ℓ)(j+1))$ and a bound
$ log |H^2| <= exp((2j[K:ℚ])^(C_1) log(2|D_K|)), $
where $C_1$ here denotes a single field-independent exponent in the coarse lemma and $D_K$ is the discriminant. The size is generous. Applying it with the full varying field at every twist would destroy the target estimate. Instead the proof passes to cyclotomic towers and combines the coarse lemma at small indices with control of the remaining specialized modules. Ferrero–Washington is used in the abelian towers to remove the $mu$ term. Finite generation and the bounds on the remaining characteristic data leave sets of exceptional indices of *bounded cardinality* for the two-primary and fixed odd-prime problems. Their individual values may depend on $X,q,d$. One takes their union before selecting a window.

This step is stronger than selecting a separate sequence of good $x$ for each prime. Two unbounded sets of good points can have empty intersection. A single finite bound on the number of exceptional indices permits an elementary simultaneous selection.

#claim[Window selection lemma][Given $r$ positive exceptional indices, $0<eta<=C_t$ and $X>1$, there exists $Y$ with $X^(1/J)<=Y$, $Y^2<=X$, where $J=2^(r+2)(C_t/eta)^r$, such that for every $x in [Y,Y^2]$ each exceptional index $h<=x^(C_t)$ also satisfies $h<=x^eta$.]

For $C_t=4$, $eta=1$ and one index $h>1$, its forbidden raw scales are exactly $[h^(1/4),h)$. At $x=h^(1/4)$ the twist already enters the cutoff. At $x=h$ it has become cheap enough to keep. The same half-open endpoint convention for all indices is what permits the selection argument below to return a *closed* good interval.

== An exceptional twist as a nearby integral point <c16s08>

The two-primary and fixed odd-prime bounds share an ultrametric mechanism. For a coefficient prime $ℓ$ the tower estimate supplies at most $R$ points $s_i$ in a normed extension of $ℚ_ℓ$. The integer $R$ depends on the fixed base data in the allowed manner. At twists well separated from every $s_i$ there is a uniform linear bound for the length in terms of the layer, the roots of unity in the base and the logarithm of the largest twist. The estimate is a quoted arithmetic input. The passage from these points to a bounded *number of integer twists* is elementary and quite sharp.

Fix $Z>=1$ and set $b=floor(log_(ℓ)(Z))$. For a point $s$ consider those integers $1<=j<=Z$ satisfying $abs(j-s)_ℓ<ℓ^(-b)$. If two such integers $j_1,j_2$ existed, the ultrametric inequality would give $abs(j_1-j_2)_ℓ<ℓ^(-b)$. A nonzero difference would then be divisible by $ℓ^(b+1)$. Its ordinary absolute value is at most $Z-1$, whereas $ℓ^(b+1)>Z$. This is impossible. Hence each point contributes at most one integer to the exceptional set. The strict inequality at the boundary is essential. If equality were admitted, distinct integers separated by $ℓ^b$ could both qualify.

#example[Locating the exceptions by congruences][Take $ℓ=3$ and $Z=70$. Then $3^3<=70<3^4$. For an integral $3$-adic root whose residue modulo $81$ is $52$, the inequality $abs(j-s)_3<3^(-3)$ is equivalent to $j equiv 52 (mod 81)$. Exactly one integer in the range qualifies. A root with residue $77$ contributes none. Two roots with the same residue still contribute only one twist. In the proof the points need not be integral or belong to $ℚ_ℓ$ itself, and the difference argument works in the larger normed field without computing any residues.]

For each input tower take the union of its near-point sets. If it has at most $R$ points, the union has at most $R$ elements. The bound holds for every $Z$. The exceptional elements themselves can change when $Z$ changes. Put $j_0=floor(log_(ℓ)(R))+3$, with the integer logarithm convention of the tower estimate when $R=0$. Away from this exceptional set, at layer number $m$ one has a bound of the shape
$ R (max(m,j_0)+c+floor(log_(ℓ)(Z))) $
on the restriction-kernel length. Here the fixed base contains a primitive $ℓ^c$th root of unity. A second bound, applicable to every twist, compares the kernel length at layer $m$ with the degree-two length at the *fixed* layer $j_0$, plus an error bounded by $R max(m-j_0,0)$. This second inequality has different arithmetic content. It trades a defect of separation from the roots against the cost of a field whose degree is controlled. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.SecondBound")[The two tower bounds.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.Exc")[From roots to exceptional integer twists.]

At the two-primary place the starting field can itself vary with $q,d$. The quoted input still gives a single bound on the number of relevant points for each fixed original abelian base. Its construction uses a number field of degree at most eight times the degree of that base and embeds it into a controlled cyclotomic extension. The field used by the proof embeds into the comparison through a layer whose depth is at most the chosen two-power index plus two, with at most an additive unit of length at the pointwise comparison. At the bad odd primes the relevant bases are fixed once $psi$ and $ℓ$ have been specified. Their number is bounded by $|B_psi|$. Ferrero–Washington, local finiteness and duality, and the coarse degree-two estimate feed the uniform tower theorem. Simply deleting the Iwasawa $mu$ term at a fixed tower would not alone establish these uniform conclusions. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.ThmBabOfLemmaK")[Arithmetic inputs for the simultaneous tower theorem.]

== Selecting one interval for every exceptional twist <c16s09>

Fix the outer scale $X>1$, the prime pair, and the finite set of towers to be controlled. For each tower, the preceding argument singles out at most its rank bound in integer twists up to the outer cutoff. Take the union $cal(E)$ of these sets. A bound for its cardinality is the two-primary rank bound plus the sum of the rank bounds for the fixed odd-prime towers. Coincident exceptional indices only decrease the size of the union. Write $R$ for this common cardinality bound. Its value can depend on the fixed primitive character. It was chosen before the scale and prime pair, whereas the set $cal(E)$ itself need only exist after they have been chosen.

The goal is to choose a whole interval on which the implication
$ h in cal(E), quad h<=x^(C_t) quad ==> quad h<=x^eta $
holds. A point to its right could cause trouble even if the implication holds at the left endpoint, because a new twist may enter the count as $x$ grows. There is an analogous problem to the left. As $x$ decreases, a twist which was cheap can remain present and become expensive. The following interval argument handles both transitions.

Use natural logarithms and put $U=log X$, $B=C_t/eta>=1$, and $a_h=(log h)/C_t$ for a positive exceptional integer $h$. The forbidden real values for $u=log x$ are
$ a_h <= u < B a_h. $
The endpoint $a_h$ belongs to the forbidden interval if $a_h<B a_h$. The endpoint $B a_h$ is permitted, since equality $h=x^eta$ is enough for the coarse estimate. If $h=1$ or $B=1$ the interval is empty. The twist $0$, should one include it in a formal finite set, is automatically good. Removing it before taking logarithms leaves the conclusion unchanged.

Consider a current upper budget $V>0$, initially $U$. If no $a_h$ lies in $(V/(2 B),V]$, put $y=V/2$. Every positive $a_h$ is then either greater than $V$ or at most $V/(2 B)$. In the former case it lies beyond every $u in [y,2y]$. In the latter case $B a_h<=V/2<=u$, so all such $u$ are safe. If some $a_h$ does lie in that half-open interval, discard it and set $V$ equal to the old value divided by $2 B$. The discarded $a_h$ strictly exceeds this new upper budget. It will remain to the right of every interval considered subsequently. At most $R$ discards are possible, whether or not one systematically discards the largest point. With $r<=R$ discards this particular procedure takes $y=U/(2(2 B)^r)$. The weaker uniform estimate stated in the release is
$ frac(U,2^(R+2) B^R)<=y, quad 2y<=U. $
Its slack can be used without identifying the actual number of discards. Exponentiating gives $Y=exp(y)$ and an interval $[Y,Y^2]$ above $X^(1/J)$ with $J=2^(R+2) B^R$. This is a relative interval on the logarithmic scale, so its ratio of endpoints also tends to infinity with $X$. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.Window1")[The logarithmic interval lemma.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.Window")[Transfer to the power cutoff.]

#example[Three explicit exceptional indices][Take $X=2^256$, $C_t=4$, $eta=1$ and $cal(E)={2^640,2^80,4}$. Work in base-two logarithms throughout this calculation. The three left endpoints are $160,20,1/2$, and their right endpoints are $640,80,2$. The first upper budget is $256$. The interval $(32,256]$ contains $160$, so remove it and shrink the budget to $32$. The interval $(4,32]$ contains $20$, so remove it and shrink the budget to $4$. The interval $(1/2,4]$ now contains none. The last left endpoint is exactly $1/2$ and is retained. Take $y=2$, giving $Y=4$ and $x in [4,16]$. The small exceptional twist $4$ can contribute everywhere on this window, but it satisfies $4<=x$. The twists $2^80$ and $2^640$ never enter, since even $16^4=2^16$ lies below both. The theorem would have allowed $J=2^5 4^3=2048$ and only required $Y>=2^(1/8)$. Exact knowledge of the positions can thus produce a much larger window than the guaranteed one.]

Returning to the original problem, take a common root cutoff through $floor(X^(C_t))$. Every twist examined at any $x$ in the selected window lies in the range used to form $cal(E)$. Outside $cal(E)$ the tower estimate applies directly. Inside it, once the twist enters the count, its size is at most $x^eta$. The interval therefore selects between two available proofs of a bound on the same twist. It need not suppress every obstruction by making the corresponding group vanish.

== Paying for the exceptional twists <c16s10>

Let $C_K>0$ be the exponent in the arithmetic estimate called Lemma K. For a totally complex number field $L$ containing the required roots of unity and $j>=1$, the degree-two cohomology of the indicated Galois group with coefficients $ℤ_(ℓ)(j+1)$ is finite and satisfies
$ log |H^2| <= exp((2 j [L:ℚ])^(C_K) log(2 abs(D_L))). $
The explicit roots-of-unity requirement is a primitive $ℓ$th root at odd $ℓ$ and a primitive fourth root at $ℓ=2$. Here $D_L$ is the discriminant of the field, and the ramification data are those of the degree-two group in the tower comparison. Finiteness is part of the arithmetic theorem. Changing from log order to length introduces $frac(1,log ℓ)<=frac(1,log 2)<2$, which explains the factor two in the length version. The tower comparison supplies a field in a fixed layer for which this coarse bound is useful. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.GeneratorCountTuples.LemmaK")[Finite degree-two groups and the quantitative estimate.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.Klen")[Passage to length.]

The coarse exponent may be increased. The base $2 j[L:ℚ]$ is at least two, so a valid exponent $C_0$ gives a valid bound with $C_K=max(C_0,1)$. Thus the specific choice $eta=frac(epsilon,2 C_K)$ is positive and at most $1<=C_t$, as required by the interval lemma. Let the field occurring in the fixed-layer estimate have degree at most $n_0$ and satisfy $log(2 abs(D_L))<=c_0 x^(epsilon/4)$. If $j<=x^eta$, then
$ (2j[L:ℚ])^(C_K) log(2 abs(D_L))
  <= (2n_0)^(C_K) c_0 x^(epsilon/2+epsilon/4). $
The two portions $epsilon/2$ and $epsilon/4$ pay for different quantities. Only the first can be made smaller by lowering the exceptional-twist cutoff. The second pays for the discriminant of the varying two-primary base. For a fixed odd-prime base its discriminant is a fixed number. The same formula thus controls both cases after enlargement of a constant. Applying the estimate at the full field would require the same calculation with a new, generally large, factor $[E:ℚ]^(C_K)$. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.ThmBabOf")[Choice of the exponent and compatibility with window selection.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.Kexp")[The bound with exponent $3epsilon/4$.]

For a twist away from the exceptional set, the factors depending on the tower level and the integer logarithm of the outer twist cutoff are bounded by a fixed multiple of $1+log x$. Indeed, $x>=X^(1/J)$ gives $log X<=J log x$, and the two-power layer satisfies $h log 2<=log 2+(2+8epsilon)log x$. A natural-number logarithm to base $ℓ>=2$ is at most the corresponding real logarithm divided by $log 2$. For an exceptional twist the second tower inequality adds a fixed multiple of the same logarithmic cost to the coarse degree-two bound just obtained. Enlarging constants, a single upper bound for either twist is of the shape
$ C(1+log x)+2 exp(C x^(3epsilon/4)). $
It is a bound on each length, accompanied by finiteness, uniform over the twists and field realizations under consideration. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.PartA")[Uniform two-primary comparison.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.PartB")[Fixed odd-prime comparisons.]

There are fewer than $t<=x^(C_t)$ positive twists below $t$. The logarithm of their total upper bound is at most $C_t log x+C' + C''x^(3epsilon/4)$ for large $x$. Dividing by $x^(4epsilon/5)$ makes each term tend to zero. For the last term this uses the fast-growing ratio $x^(epsilon/20)$, however small the fixed $epsilon$ is. We may therefore use $exp(x^(4epsilon/5))$ as the upper bound for the *sum* of the lengths in each required tower. It is larger than the pointwise bound for a reason. Multiplying by a polynomial number of twists has already been paid for before this number is substituted into the character count. The strict gap between the two exponents permits a common lower threshold independent of the outer scale. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.Vvat")[The summation over twists.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.LayeredIsomorphisms.Final")[Absorbing the polynomial cutoff.]

== Putting the bounds on one interval <c16s11>

On the selected interval the two-primary and finite-layer estimates give bounds of size $exp(x^(4epsilon/5))$ in the intermediate counting statements. The fixed power of $N t$ is controlled there as well. Its logarithm is bounded in terms of $log(q d)$, the two-power level, the original conductor and $log t$. The pair conditions bound $log(q d)$ on a strictly smaller power scale than $x^epsilon$, and $log t<=401 log x$. For fixed constants and sufficiently large $x$ the total allowance fits under $exp(x^epsilon/2)=sqrt(b)$. In particular the cohomological argument permits finite nonzero restriction kernels. A claim of vanishing for every group in the sum would be much stronger than the arithmetic comparison requires.

== Collecting the conductor, minus, and two-primary terms <c16s12>

Write $P=X^(1/J)$ and $z=P^(epsilon/5)$. The prime pair at this scale satisfies $log q<=z$ and $log d<=z(log P)^2$, with strict lower bounds half as large. For large $P$,
$ log(q d)<=P^(epsilon/5) (1+(log P)^2)<=P^(epsilon/4). $
The last inequality follows by dividing by $P^(epsilon/5)$ and using the positive exponent $epsilon/20$. Conversely $log q>z/2$ eventually gives $q>2 X^(C_t)+1$, since $z$ grows like a fixed positive power of $X$ and the logarithm of the latter quantity grows like $log X$. The same reasoning applies to $d$. The upper bounds on the pair control the conductor, while these lower bounds put the special primes beyond all the small-prime exceptions for every twist in the outer range. The congruences selecting the pair, including $q$ dividing $d+1$, are additional arithmetic hypotheses. The two size calculations themselves use only the displayed logarithmic windows. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.CMFieldExponentialBounds.logpair")[Upper bound for the product.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.CMFieldExponentialBounds.bigpair")[Lower bounds compared with the entire twist range.]

On the selected interval $x>=P$, the bound on $log(q d)$ is at most $x^(epsilon/4)$. We may use a conductor bound $N<=2^(h+2)M q d$, with $2^h<=2x^(2+8epsilon)$. Since $t<=x^(C_t)$, the remaining factors of $N t$ have combined logarithm bounded by a constant plus a fixed multiple of $log x$. Hence, on raising the common lower threshold,
$ log(N t)<=2x^(epsilon/4). $
This argument explains how the large primes needed for separation are consistent with a small log conductor. There is ample space between a number growing polynomially in $X$ and an exponential in a positive power of $P$. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.CMFieldExponentialBounds.LogConductorMulHeightLeOfPageN")[Uniform bound for log conductor times height.]

Let $d_E=[E:ℚ]$ and let $o$ be the degree of the fixed abelian character datum in the minus estimate. The relevant prime divisors of $o$ do not vary with $E$. The explicit bound, with a fixed constant $C$, is
$ 3 d_E t^2 log(t N)
   + C sum_(ℓ divides o, ℓ != 2) ℓ d_E^2 t^2 (log(t ℓ N))^2. $
The index runs over odd prime divisors. Put $v=N t>=1$. Since $d_E<=N$, one has $d_E t<=v$ and $t<=v$. Also $0<=log v<=v$ and $0<=log(ℓ v)<=ℓ v$. The first term is at most $3v^4$, and each summand in the other term before multiplication by $C$ is at most $ℓ^3 v^4$. Hence the whole expression is at most $K v^4<=K exp(8x^(epsilon/4))$, where one may take $K=3+|C|sum_(ℓ divides o, ℓ!=2) ℓ^3$. This argument uses the degree $d_E$ of the entire field. For the realized host it is $4n$, whereas $n=|H|$ and $f=|Phi|=2n$ are the counts used by the Fourier normalization. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.StmtFields")[The fixed abelian datum and minus bound.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.CMFieldExponentialBounds.minusle")[The explicit polynomial majorant.]

The two-primary subtotal is now at most $(log 2)exp(x^(4epsilon/5))$. The plus subtotal is at most $v^C + C exp(x^(4epsilon/5))$ by the finite-layer implication, and the preceding paragraph controls the minus subtotal. For a direct comparison with the allowed budget write $B_x=exp(x^epsilon/2)$. Dividing the logarithm of each nonzero cost by $x^epsilon$ gives a quantity tending to zero, since all powers which occur are at most $4epsilon/5<epsilon$. For example $log(v^C)<=2 C x^(epsilon/4)$, and $log(K exp(8x^(epsilon/4)))=log K+8x^(epsilon/4)$ when $K>0$. Each fixed multiple of a subsidiary cost is therefore at most $B_x/4$ on a sufficiently late common range. Adding the four displayed types of cost yields $sqrt(exp(x^epsilon))$ for the whole subtotal. At this point all primes in the arbitrary finite set and every positive twist below $t$ have been accounted for. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.AutomorphicVarieties.CMFieldExponentialBounds.ErrorTermsLeSqrtExpRpow")[Final absorption with precisely these three sources of cost.]

== From a raw interval to a field at a physical point <c16s13>

The conclusion of this chapter concerns $[W,W^2]$ in the raw variable $x'$. For a fixed target the order of the auxiliary group $H$ is $n=c dot 2^h$, with $h=e(x')$ and fixed $c>=1$. The full Galois group has order $4n$. Chapter 18 chooses a physical interval on which the same $h$ gives the required ranks and fields. The finite-subtotal estimate just proved applies to every field satisfying its fixed-field condition, which allows it to be specialized to the field selected by the geometric construction.

Chapter 17 first works at one fixed physical $x$ and on one such host. It averages over translates there to recover an inequality for the target function at $x$. Chapter 18 then supplies these hosts for every point of a sufficiently late window and applies the analytic zero criterion. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.FieldChi")[The multiplier in the order of the auxiliary group.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.ShortNorms.supplyOfAdmOf")[The common host and its cohomology bound.]

